Genotoxicity in Rats Treated With the Antidiabetic
Agent, Rosiglitazone
Abdulkerim Bedir,1* Yuksel Aliyazicioglu,1 Hakki Kahraman,2
Zafer Yurdakul,1 Mehmet Uysal,1 Duygu Erol Suvaci,1 Ali Okuyucu,1
Murat H˛kelek,3 and Muhlise Alvur1
1

Department of Biochemistry, Faculty of Medicine, Ondokuz Mayis University,
55139 Samsun, Turkey
2
Department of Endocrinology, Faculty of Medicine, Ondokuz Mayis University,
55139 Samsun, Turkey
3
Department of Microbiology, Faculty of Medicine, Ondokuz Mayis University,
55139 Samsun, Turkey
Rosiglitazone (RSG), a member of the thiazolidinedione class of antidiabetic agents, improves glycemic control by increasing insulin sensitivity. The therapeutic mode of action of RSG involves its activity
as a highly selective and potent agonist for peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor-g. Although
other drugs in this class have displayed unacceptable hepatotoxicity, RSG was approved for human
use. The package insert indicates that RSG has minimal genotoxicity, but information on the genotoxicity of RSG is not available in the published literature.
In this study, we used the single cell gel electrophoresis (SCGE)/Comet assay to investigate the DNA
damage in peripheral blood and liver cells of rats
treated with RSG. Sixteen male Sprague-Dawley
rats were randomly distributed into four groups, and
dosed daily by oral gavage with 0.0, 0.5, 1.0, and
2.0 mg/kg/day RSG. The rats dosed with 2.0 mg/
kg/day RSG received an 10-times the area under

the curve concentration of the maximum recommended human daily dose. After 14 days of treatment, the rats were euthanized, and peripheral
blood and liver were collected and processed for
the Comet assay. A dose-dependent increase in
DNA damage (as assessed by % tail DNA and
Olive Tail Moment) was observed in the hepatocytes of RSG-treated groups, with signiﬁcant increases detected between rats treated with all the
doses of RSG and the control, and between rats
treated with different RSG doses (P &lt; 0.05 – P &lt;
0.0001). In contrast, DNA damage was detected in
peripheral blood lymphocytes only in rats treated
with the higher RSG doses (1.0 and 2 mg/kg/day).
Taken together, the data indicate that RSG is able to
induce primary DNA damage in rats, with greater
damage being detected in liver cells than lymphocytes. Environ. Mol. Mutagen. 47:718–724,
2006. VC 2006 Wiley-Liss, Inc.

Key words: genotoxicity; Rosiglitazone; comet assay; single cell gel electrophoresis

INTRODUCTION
Thiazolidinediones (TZDs) are a new class of oral antidiabetic agents that directly target insulin resistance
[Sheen, 1997; Grossman et al., 1997; Day, 1999]. Drugs
of this class act as ligands for the g subtype of the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor (PPARg), which is
directly involved in the regulation of genes controlling
glucose homeostasis and lipid metabolism [Saltiel and
Olefsky, 1996; Spiegelman, 1998]. PPARg is a member
of the steroid/thyroid nuclear hormone receptor superfamily [Evans, 1988] and plays an important role in cellular
physiology and metabolism. Because of its physiological
role in glucose and lipid metabolism, many natural and
synthetic agonists of PPARg are used to treat adult-onset
noninsulin-dependent diabetic patients [Day, 1999; Levovitz et al., 2002].
C 2006 Wiley-Liss, Inc.
V

The clinical development of TZDs was initially delayed
because of poor efﬁcacy or unacceptable toxicity, which
led to discontinuation of ciglitazone and englitazone after
the Phase II clinical trials [Day, 1999]. Three compounds,
troglitazone [Spencer and Merkham, 1997; Plosker and
Faulds, 1999], rosiglitazone (RSG) [Balfour and Plosker,
1999], and pioglitazone [Gillies and Dunn, 2000],

*Correspondence to: Abdulkerim Bedir, Department of Biochemistry,
Faculty of Medicine, Ondokuz Mayis University, 55139 Samsun, Turkey.
E-mail: abedir@omu.edu.tr
Received 27 May 2006; provisionally accepted 30 June 2006; and in
ﬁnal form 11 August 2006
DOI 10.1002/em.20261
Published online 31 October 2006 in Wiley InterScience (www.interscience.
wiley.com).

Environmental and Molecular Mutagenesis. DOI 10.1002/em
Rosiglitazone Genotoxicity

719

otoxicity of RSG, and the lack of in vivo genotoxicity
data in particular, the present study was designed to evaluate whether this drug can cause DNA damage in the
hepatocytes and peripheral blood lymphocytes of treated
rats. DNA damage was measured using the single cell gel
electrophoresis assay (Comet assay), a method that is used
widely for the detection of DNA damage both in vivo and
in vitro [Tice et al., 2000; Hartmann et al., 2003].

MATERIALS AND METHODS
Chemicals
RSG (CAS 122320-73-4) was purchased from Cayman Chemical
Company (Ann Arbor, MI). All other chemicals were of analytical reagent grade, and obtained from Sigma (St. Louis, MO).

Animals
Fig. 1. Structures and molecular formulas of troglitazone (A) and rosiglitazone (B).

appeared to have acceptable toxicity proﬁles in clinical
trials and subsequently were approved for human use by
the US Food and Drug Administration. The thiazolidine2-4-dione structure is common to all drugs of this class
(Fig. 1), the difference lying in their side chains, which
may modify the pharmacological activity and side effects.
Soon after its launch in 1997, the ﬁrst available TZD, troglitazone, was associated with hepatotoxicity, with the
report of the several dozen cases of severe liver failure
and death [Watkins and Whitcomb, 1998; Kohlroser
et al., 2000]. These toxicities led to its withdrawal from
the US market in March 2000.
Controlled clinical trials have demonstrated RSG’s efﬁcacy in improving blood glucose control in Type-II diabetes patients being treated with diet alone [Patel et al.,
1999], or with sulphanylurea [Wolffenbuttel et al., 2000],
metformin [Fonseca et al., 2000] or insulin [Balfour
et al., 1999]. Recently, a report suggested that RSG may
cause hepatotoxicity in humans [Forman et al., 2000],
although the incidence of RSG-related hepatotoxicity is
considered extremely low [Levovitz et al., 2002]. Additional studies have revealed that RSG causes cell growth
arrest or death in normal and cancerous cells [Sugimura
et al., 1999; Gouni-Berthold et al., 2001]. Together, these
results suggest the possibility that RSG may damage cells
of hepatic origin.
It is becoming increasingly evident that an increased
rate of DNA damage and chromosome breakage or loss is
an important risk factor for cancer [Bonassi et al., 2000].
A variety of in vitro and in vivo test systems is available
for evaluating the early genetic damage induced by xenobiotics. Considering the lack of published data on the gen-

Male Sprague-Dawley rats were obtained from our breeding colony at
Samsun; rats were used for the experiments at 7–8 weeks of age
(weighing 248–275 g). Permission for the animal studies was obtained
from the University Ethics Committee, and the experiments were performed according to their guidelines for the care and use of laboratory
animals.
The rats were housed singly in cages with wood bedding in a temperature-regulated room under conventional conditions ((22 6 3)8C, 40–60%
humidity, and an artiﬁcial light cycle of 12-hr light and 12-hr dark). All
animals were given free access to tap water and standard commercial
pellet diet (Samsun Yem Company, Samsun, Turkey).
The animals were randomly divided into four groups (4 rats in each
group) and treated as follows: Group IV: vehicle control; Group I: 2.0 mg/
kg RSG; Group II: 1.0 mg/kg RSG; and Group III: 0.5 mg/kg RSG.

Drug Administration
RSG was ﬁrst dissolved in DMSO and then diluted with phosphatebuffered saline (PBS) (pH 7.4). The ﬁnal DMSO concentration in the solution was 3.0% (v/v). Rats were dosed once a day for 14 days by oral
gavage with doses of 0.5, 1.0, and 2.0 mg/kg of body weight RSG [exposure (AUC(0–24)) at the highest dose equivalent to 10-times the
human therapeutic exposure at the maximum recommended human daily
dose of 8 mg] between 09:00 and 09:30 hr every morning (http://
www.fda.gov/cder/foi/label/1999). Control rats received an equal volume
(4 mL/kg) of the vehicle (3.0% DMSO in PBS).

Blood Sampling and Lymphocyte Isolation
At the end of 14 days, and 4 hr after the last treatment, all the rats
were killed by decapitation under a short ether anesthesia. Blood was
collected into tubes containing EDTA by heart puncture for the Comet
assay using lymphocytes. All blood samples were processed immediately
after sampling. After layering 1.5 mL of blood over 1 mL of Histopaque-1077 (Sigma), rat lymphocytes were isolated by centrifugation at
1500g for 20 min at 48C. Lymphocyte yield was determined with a cell
counter (Gen S; Beckman-Coulter, Fullerton, CA) and adjusted to 1 3
103 cells/lL by dilution with PBS. The yield of cells was &gt;85%
throughout the assays. The cell suspension was used immediately for the
Comet assay.

Environmental and Molecular Mutagenesis. DOI 10.1002/em
720

Bedir et al.

Chelation Method for Liver Cell Isolation
Liver was immediately dissected out of the euthanized rats, and
washed in ice-cold phosphate-buffered salt solution (PBSS) to remove
the blood. Liver specimens were immediately placed in ice-cold Hanks
balanced salt solution (HBSS) containing no Mgþ2 or Caþ2 ions. Scalpel-minced specimens were incubated in HBSS in the presence of 2 mM
ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) and 1 mM ethylene glycolbis(2-aminoethyl ether)-N,N,N0 ,N0 -tetraacetic acid (EGTA) for 60 min at
378C. After the EDTA/EGTA treatment, the tissue specimens were separated mechanically by repeated pipetting through a 21 ga. steel needle.
The isolated liver cells were washed and centrifuged three times (600g
at 48C) to remove debris.

Cell Viability
Cell viability was evaluated by the Trypan blue dye-exclusion assay.
Immediately after isolation, the cells were washed with PBS and mixed
with equal volumes of 0.2% Trypan blue solution. The cells were examined in a counting chamber with a light microscope. Viable and nonviable cells were recorded. Each assay was performed in triplicate.

Comet Assay
The entire procedure was conducted under low indirect incandescent
light and at a temperature of 2–88C to minimize artifactual damage to
the cells. The assay was performed according to Singh et al. [1988], with
some modiﬁcations. Microscope slides were cleaned with 100% methanol, air-dried, and coated with polylysine. Two solutions, 0.5% normal
melting-point agarose (NMA) and 0.75% low melting-point agarose
(LMA), were prepared in Caþ2, Mgþ2-free PBS. 110 lL NMA was ﬁrst
layered onto the slides, allowed to solidify, and followed by a second
layer containing 65 lL LMAþ 10 lL PBS cell suspension (ca. 1 3 104
cells). Finally, a third layer of 75 lL NMA was added. The slides were
immersed in an ice-cold, freshly prepared solution (2.5 M NaCl, 100
mM Na2EDTA, 10 mM Tris-HCl [pH 10.0], and 1% Triton X-100) to
lyse the cells and to allow DNA unwinding. After 1 hr at 48C in the
dark, the slides were placed in a horizontal electrophoresis unit and covered with chilled alkaline electrophoresis solution (0.3 M NaOH, 10 mM
EDTA, pH 13.1). After the slides remained in this solution for 30 min,
electrophoresis was conducted for 30 min at 25 V (1.5 V/cm) and
300 mA. Following electrophoresis, the slides were washed in neutralizing buffer (0.4 M Tris, pH 7.5).
Comet assay analysis. The dried microscope slides were stained with
50 lL of 20 lg/mL ethidium bromide, covered with a cover-glass, and
examined at 1003 using a Nikon E800 ﬂuorescence microscope. The
microscope was connected to a charge-coupled device (CCD) camera
and a PC-image analysis program, Computer Assay Software Project
(CASP, version 1.2.2), developed by Konca et al. [2003]. The relative
intensity of staining in comet tails was measured as an indication of
DNA damage [Collins, 2004], and used to compute % tail DNA (percent
of DNA in the comet tail) and Olive tail moment (OTM) ([percent of
DNA in the tail] x [distance between the center of gravity of DNA in
the tail and the center of gravity of DNA in the head in the 3-direction])
[Green et al., 1996].
Evaluation of slides. Three slides per tissue were processed for each animal. Fifty cells from each of the slides were scored for DNA damage.
The scoring of slides was performed on a \blind&quot; basis, with the scorer
being unaware of the treatment conditions for each sample.

Statistical Analysis
All analysis was performed using SPSS for Windows (version 10.0)
(SPSS, Chicago, IL). The Kolmogorov-Smirnoff test established that neither of the comet parameters was normally distributed. Data analysis

was performed using parametric tests according to the central limit theorem. Comparison of the control (Group IV) and the treatment groups
(Group I through Group III) was performed by one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA). A two-way ANOVA analysis was performed for effects
of dose and cell type on DNA damage expressed as % tail DNA. The
Bonferroni test was used for pair-wise comparisons of the groups. Values
are given as the mean 6 SD. Statistical signiﬁcance was set at P &lt; 0.05.

RESULTS
Effects of Isolation Procedures on Cell Viability
The effects on cell viability of the Ficoll density gradient and chelation methods used for isolating peripheral
blood lymphocytes and liver cells, respectively, were
measured by Trypan blue dye-exclusion. All samples displayed cell viability greater than 98%.
Genotoxicity Studies
The Comet assay used to measure the genotoxicity of
RSG complied with published guidelines [Tice et al., 2000;
Hartmann et al., 2003] and employed % tail DNA and OTM
as measures of DNA damage. Under ﬂuorescence microscopy, cells from control rats had tightly compressed DNA
and maintained the circular form of the normal nucleus, with
little or no evidence of comet formation. In contrast, cells
from rats dosed with RSG displayed an altered appearance,
with ﬂuorescent streaks extending from the nucleus in the
form of comets.
The background level of damage, expressed as % tail
DNA, in the liver cells and lymphocytes of untreated rats
ranged from 1.34 to 2.01% and 0.29 to 0.42% [95% conﬁdence interval (CI) for the mean], respectively. Comparison of the mean % tail DNA and OTM for the control
rats with the treated groups indicated that RSG produced
dose-responsive increases in DNA damage in both the
liver cells and peripheral lymphocytes; however, only the
two higher doses signiﬁcantly increased the DNA damage
in lymphocytes, while all three doses produced signiﬁcant
increases in liver (Table I). At the same dose of RSG,
comets from liver cells displayed a considerably higher
mean % tail DNA and OTM than did comets from peripheral lymphocytes. Even the lowest dose of RSG tested
(0.5/kg/day) produced a mean of 3.85% for % tail DNA
in liver (Table I). Two-way ANOVA analysis showed that
dose of RSG and cell type were the main factors (P &lt;
0.001 and P &lt; 0.001, respectively) that affected % tail
DNA, and there was a signiﬁcant interaction between
them, indicating that the liver response was greater than
the lymphocyte response (P &lt; 0.001).
Figures 2 and 3 show the frequency distributions of %
tail DNA in the liver cells and blood lymphocytes, respectively, of rats treated with 0.0, 0.5, 1.0, and 2.0 mg/kg/
day RSG for 14 days. The percentage of cells with larger
% tail DNA values increased as the dose of RSG in-

