shown).
DISCUSSION
The Comet assay is a rapid and inexpensive method for
measuring DNA single strand breaks. The Comet assay

also is considered by at least some investigators to be
more sensitive than other assays for DNA damage, such
as the sister chromatid exchange, alkaline elution, and
micronucleus assays [Lee and Steinert, 2003]. We used
the alkaline version of the Comet assay (pH &gt; 13), as
described by Singh et al. [1988]. At pH &gt; 13, increased
DNA migration is associated with increased levels of
frank DNA single-strand breaks (SSBs), excision repair
sites, and alkali-labile sites (e.g., apurinic sites). An
expert panel on genetic toxicology testing strategy indicated that the alkaline version was the method of choice
for the Comet assay [Tice et al., 2000].

Environmental and Molecular Mutagenesis. DOI 10.1002/em
722

Bedir et al.

Fig. 3. Histograms showing the distribution of % tail DNA in the blood lymphocytes of rats given RSG.

Type-II diabetes is multifactorial disease, and diverse
types of pharmaceutical agents have been employed for
its treatment, including agents that control glucose levels,
improve insulin production or utilization, or compensate
for alterations of lipid and carbohydrate metabolism
[National Diabetes Data Group, 1987; Kahn et al., 2000].
TZDs, in general, have similar therapeutic proﬁles, albeit
with different relative potencies for the management of
diabetics [Kameda et al., 2000]. TZDs are therapeutically
effective in Type-II diabetes and more than 1.5 million
diabetics are prescribed these drugs. There is, however,
no published information on the genotoxicity of this class
of drugs.
In particular, there are no in vivo assays for the genotoxic potential of RSG reported in the literature. Age,
gender, dietary habits, and smoking status are confounding factors in the human use of the drug [Moller, 2006].
Although dietary habits have been long associated with
increased risk for mutation-related diseases such as cancer
[Collins, 1999], it is very difﬁcult to evaluate their effects
in biomonitoring studies on human populations. Thus, the
current study was conducted using an animal model,
which makes possible the evaluation of the effects of
RSG under controlled conditions.
The toxicity of the related TZD, troglitazone, has been
attributed to the formation of a reactive quinone metabolite and to a quinone methide conjugate [Kassahun et al.,
2001]. Since RSG does not have the a-tocopherol moiety
that produces the reactive quinone metabolites from troglitazone [Levovitz et al., 2002], it is unlikely that RSG

would be toxic through a similar mechanism (Fig. 1).
According to data obtained by coherent multiprobe ﬂuorescence assays [Haskins et al., 2001], RSG decreased
transmembrane potential in both liver cells and peripheral
blood mononuclear cells, although to a lesser extent than
troglitazone, and caused a fall in intracellular ATP (mitochondrial uncoupling). Moreover, the early onset of mitochondrial uncoupling and transmembrane potential loss
caused by TZDs precede the appearance of metabolic biotransformation products that are detected in the bile. In
light of these data, RSG itself may cause disruption of
mitochondrial activity and cellular integrity, thus negatively affecting oxidative metabolite formation in an
ATP-depleted and uncoupled cellular environment. In
vitro data demonstrate that RSG is metabolized predominantly by cytochrome P450 (CYP) isoenzyme 2C8, with
CYP2C9 contributing as a minor pathway (http://www.
fda.gov/cder/foi/label/1999). Taken together, increased oxidant stress resulting from the uncoupled cellular environment and CYP-mediated oxidation reactions may result in
the generation of free radicals resulting not only in cellular toxicity (e.g., hepatotoxicity), but also genotoxicity.
The package insert for RSG (http://www.fda.gov/cder/
foi/label/1999) indicates that its genotoxic potential was
evaluated in a series of in vitro and in vivo assays. RSG
was equivocal in the mouse lymphoma gene-mutation
assay conducted with the microtiter technique, and was
negative in the agar-plate method. RSG was negative in
bacterial assays for gene mutation, the in vitro chromosome aberration test in human lymphocytes, the in vivo

Environmental and Molecular Mutagenesis. DOI 10.1002/em
Rosiglitazone Genotoxicity

mouse micronucleus test, and in the in vivo/in vitro rat
UDS assay. The equivocal response in the mouse lymphoma assay was due to an approximate two-fold increase
in mutation in the presence of metabolic activation.
The Comet assay has elucidated some of the molecular
changes caused by RSG administration in the rat. As seen
in the dose-response data from the present study, the
extent of DNA damage increased proportionately with
increasing doses of RSG, especially in liver. Possibly,
owing to processing artifacts or low levels of DNA repair,
untreated control cells consistently exhibited a small
amount of DNA in the tail that did not exceed 10.46%
(97.5th percentile) and 1.81% (97.5th percentile) of the
total DNA in liver cells and peripheral lymphocytes,
respectively. Since peripheral blood mononuclear cells
have baseline mitochondrial energetics and no RSG metabolism in comparison with hepatocytes [Haskins et al.,
2001; Furukawa et al., 2004], the differences between
their comets may be due to oxidative DNA damage produced by the drug-metabolizing processes.
In conclusion, the present study indicates that exposure
of rats to RSG results in DNA damage in liver cells, and,
to a lesser extent, in peripheral blood lymphocytes. These
data indicate that there is a potential health risk associated with the use of these drugs and indicate the need for
a better understanding of the mechanism of RSG genotoxicity.

